KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ defaulted three-way comparison: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
g++ and clang++ (GCC 16 for C++23 and C++26) accept this program, and it fails
only its WRONG assertion.  `a < b' works (rewritten to (a <=> b) < 0), but `a
== b' fails to type-check: "no comparison operator for an operand of class
type". [class.compare.default]/5: a defaulted operator<=> also declares a
defaulted operator== (with the same parameters), which CBMC does not.  A
separately defaulted operator== works.
